WEBVTT

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:10.230
<v Ira Glass>When Dave was turning his life around and trying different things and figuring out what to do next, one of the places he looked was to the Howard Stern Show. And I think that was a really good choice.
<v Ira Glass>当戴夫（Dave）试图改变人生，尝试各种新鲜事物并思考下一步该做什么时，他求助的地方之一就是霍华德·斯特恩（Howard Stern）的节目。我觉得那是个非常明智的选择。

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:35.160
<v Ira Glass>I know everybody is not a fan of Howard Stern, but I am. And I think that one of the best things about the show is that it's always been a place where people talk with this kind of brutal and funny honesty about themselves. One of Howard's innovations is that he took the strain of crude, dark, confessional comedy that's always been a big part of stand-up, ever since Lenny Bruce-- Howard figured out a way to turn that into radio, daily radio.
<v Ira Glass>我知道并不是每个人都喜欢霍华德·斯特恩，但我喜欢。我认为该节目最棒的一点在于，它一直是一个人们能以那种残酷而滑稽的坦诚来谈论自己的地方。霍华德的创新之处在于，他汲取了自莱尼·布鲁斯（Lenny Bruce）以来单口喜剧中那种粗俗、阴暗且带有忏悔性质的特质，并想出了一种将其实现在日常广播中的方法。

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:52.830
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, to get back to this guy Dave, he loved that about the show. And in 2015, he was a few months sober, in his early 40s, working as a waiter at Katz's Deli in New York City. When he was younger, he'd worked in television, hosting an interview show for college kids but ruined that career with heroin addiction.
<v Ira Glass>总之，回到戴夫这个家伙身上，他非常喜欢该节目的这一点。2015年，四十出头的他已戒毒几个月，在纽约市的卡茨熟食店（Katz's Deli）当服务员。年轻时，他曾在电视行业工作，主持过一档针对大学生的访谈节目，但后来因海洛因成瘾毁掉了那份事业。

00:00:52.830 --> 00:01:06.970
<v Ira Glass>Now sober in recovery, he needed a project. And he decided to a podcast about drugs with the feeling of the Howard Stern Show. Like, he remembered the years when comedian Artie Lange was on Howard Stern-- definitely my favorite period of Howard Stern.
<v Ira Glass>如今在康复中，他需要一个项目。他决定做一档以药物为主题、带有霍华德·斯特恩节目风格的播客。他记得喜剧演员阿蒂·朗（Artie Lange）在霍华德·斯特恩节目组的那些年——那绝对是我最喜欢的霍华德·斯特恩时期。

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:26.280
<v Ira Glass>When Artie would come on every day. He would talk about his own drug problems. He would tell crazy stories. He would talk in this very real way about the mistakes he was making in his life at that time that he was not able to stop, and Dave wanted to shoot for that. He would co-host the show with his friend Chris who-- he had met Chris years before in rehab.
<v Ira Glass>阿蒂每天都会上节目。他会谈论自己的药物问题，讲述疯狂的故事。他会以一种非常真实的方式谈论他当时在生活中所犯的、无法停止的错误，而戴夫也想朝着这个方向努力。他将与好友克里斯（Chris）共同主持这档节目，他多年前在康复中心遇到了克里斯。

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:42.660
<v Ira Glass>They would spend hours in rehab smoking cigarettes and talking about the crazy things that they'd done on drugs. That's what they wanted the podcast to be. People'd come on, tell wild drug stories, and-- very important to Dave-- no recovery talk, which Dave found to be sanctimonious and annoying.
<v Ira Glass>他们在康复中心花了数小时抽烟，谈论他们在吸毒时做过的疯狂事。这就是他们希望播客呈现的样子。人们会来讲述狂野的药物故事，而且对戴夫来说非常重要的一点是，没有关于康复的话题，因为戴夫觉得那些话虚伪又烦人。

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.680
<v Dave>This show is not necessarily about recovery.
<v Dave>这个节目不一定与康复有关。

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.580
<v Chris>Mm-hmm.
<v Chris>嗯哼。

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:48.420
<v Dave>It's about drugs.
<v Dave>而是关于药物。

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.310
<v Chris>Maybe eventually it will be who knows.
<v Chris>也许最终会有关吧，谁知道呢。

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:55.590
<v Dave>If this show is ever about recovery, you got to get somebody else in this spot. I'm out.
<v Dave>如果这个节目要谈康复，你得找别人来做。我退出。

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:01.410
<v Ira Glass>OK, in case this isn't clear. In these recordings, Dave is the one with the deeper voice and the loud opinions, Chris is the other guy.
<v Ira Glass>好的，为了防止没说清楚。在这些录音中，声音较深沉、意见较响亮的是戴夫，另一个人是克里斯。

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:07.920
<v Dave>This show is about drug stories. It's not about recovery. It's not about doing the next right thing. It's about the last wrong thing.
<v Dave>这个节目讲的是药物故事，不是关于康复，也不是关于做接下来的正确之事，而是关于最后那件错事。

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.030
<v Chris>But maybe with the evolution of your recovery, that's what the show will become.
<v Chris>但也许随着你康复进程的发展，节目会变成那样。

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:28.190
<v Ira Glass>Notice how at the end there, Chris is not agreeing with Dave. Chris is 10 years younger than Dave, but started drugs and drinking at an earlier age-- he was just 11 or 12-- and has way more extreme stories about it, including a year in prison. He's also spent way more time in rehab.
<v Ira Glass>注意看，最后克里斯并不赞同戴夫。克里斯比戴夫小10岁，但更早就开始吸毒和酗酒——那时他才11或12岁——关于这些他有更极端的经历，包括坐了一年牢。他在康复中心待的时间也长得多。

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:46.470
<v Ira Glass>And at this point, he'd been sober for nearly two years versus four months for Dave. And all of that gave him a different feeling about recovery and sobriety. He was game to talk about it, but Dave was adamant. Sobriety isn't all that entertaining. And first and foremost, he wanted the podcast to be entertaining.
<v Ira Glass>此时，他已经戒毒近两年，而戴夫才四个月。所有这些让他对康复和清醒有了不同的感觉。他愿意谈论这个话题，但戴夫态度坚决。清醒本身并没有那么有趣，而他首先希望播客是好玩的。

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.350
<v Dave>If this show was about recovery, it's going to be lame.
<v Dave>如果这节目讲康复，那肯定很无聊。

00:02:49.350 --> 00:03:04.930
<v Ira Glass>So they'd tell drug stories. It wasn't meant to be good for you. It was meant to be fun. They listed it in the comedy section of iTunes, not self-help. If you want self-help, Dave had said, go to a meeting.
<v Ira Glass>所以他们讲述药物故事。这本来就不是为了让你变得更好，而是为了娱乐。他们把它列在iTunes的喜剧类目下，而不是自助类目。如果想自助，戴夫曾说过，去参加互助会吧。

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:22.150
<v Ira Glass>They were going to call the show War Stories, but there was already a podcast called War Stories. It was about actual war with tanks and guns and stuff, so they came up with Dopey, which really captures the spirit of the show perfectly. As Chris put it, two dopes talking about dope.
<v Ira Glass>他们原本打算把节目叫《战争故事》（War Stories），但已经有个播客叫这个名字了。那是讲关于坦克、枪支之类的真实战争，于是他们想出了《Dopey》，这完美捕捉到了节目的精神。正如克里斯所说，两个瘾君子（Dope）在谈论毒品（Dope）。

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:38.190
<v Chris>Listen, I am so grateful to be sober. And I'm so grateful to be able to talk about drugs, you know, like in a way where I'm not feeling bad about it. It's not even euphoric recall. It's just funny.
<v Chris>听着，我非常感激自己能保持清醒。我也很感激能谈论毒品，以一种不感到难过的方式。这甚至不是什么欣快回忆，纯粹是好笑而已。

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:47.550
<v Dave>And I think that's why we got along, 'cause we felt safe doing that with each other. You know, sometimes you feel a little judged with people when you recount funny stuff that's happened to you.
<v Dave>我觉得这就是我们合得来的原因，因为我们在彼此面前感到安全。你知道，有时候当你讲述发生在自己身上的趣事时，你会觉得被别人评判。

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.220
<v Chris>Yeah.
<v Chris>没错。

00:03:48.220 --> 00:04:10.030
<v Ira Glass>In Dopey, there's going to be no judgment of drug use, past or present. They recorded the first episode on a laptop, no microphone or anything, near a fish tank in Dave's apartment in Manhattan. Maybe you could hear the hum of the tank in that recording. But between this episode, Episode 1 and Episode 143 of Dopey-- just 2 and 1/2 years later-- their lives are going to change drastically.
<v Ira Glass>在《Dopey》里，不会对过去或现在的吸毒行为进行评判。他们在曼哈顿戴夫公寓的一个鱼缸旁，用一台笔记本电脑录制了第一集，没有麦克风或其他设备。也许你在那段录音里能听到鱼缸的嗡嗡声。但在这一集、第一集和第143集之间——仅仅两年半后——他们的生活将发生剧变。

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:28.660
<v Ira Glass>By Episode 143, Dopey would sound completely different. In the episodes in between, they chronicle this story where so much happens to them and some others, things lots of drug users face. But because podcasts and radio are so intimate, it all gets documented in the most unfiltered way.
<v Ira Glass>到第143集时，《Dopey》听起来将完全不同。在中间的这些集里，他们记录了一个故事，其中发生了很多事，涉及他们和一些其他人，这些都是许多吸毒者会面临的问题。但因为播客和广播是如此私密，这一切都以最不加修饰的方式被记录了下来。

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:48.490
<v Ira Glass>Dave and Chris wanted their show to be just for fun, but it ended up being so much more. Today, we're going to have two stories like this. Stories about a kind of DIY radio-- very different kinds of DIY radio-- but in both stories, it's amateurs inventing how to talk about what it is that they're going through.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫和克里斯原本只想做个纯娱乐节目，但它最终成了更多东西。今天，我们将讲述两个这样的故事。关于某种DIY广播的故事——虽然是非常不同类型的DIY广播——但在这两个故事里，都是业余人士在发明如何谈论他们正在经历的一切。

00:04:48.490 --> 00:05:20.290
<v Ira Glass>And really, both stories are dispatches from two very different battlefields. I know that sounds a little grand, but really this story-- from the opioid epidemic-- and then the other story is from a small town in Syria, in the middle of the war that's going on there. And we hear about life during that war in this way that is much more personal and close up than we usually get. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>实际上，这两个故事都是来自两个截然不同的战场的前线报道。我知道这听起来有点夸张，但确实，一个故事来自阿片类药物流行危机，另一个来自叙利亚战争中的小镇。我们以一种比平时更贴近、更个人化的方式听到了战争中的生活。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:37.490
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Two Dope Kings. So OK, you remember the premise? Two guys-- Dave and Chris-- they have this podcast. It's called Dopey. Dana Chivvis from our staff, listened to dozens of hours of the podcast, spanning 2 and 1/2 years, and tells the story of what happened.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，两个毒品之王。好吧，还记得前提吗？两个家伙——戴夫和克里斯——他们有个播客，叫《Dopey》。我们团队的达娜·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）听了数十个小时的播客，跨度两年半，并讲述了后来发生的故事。

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:49.750
<v Ira Glass>Quick warning before we start, since this is the podcast internet version of our show, we have removed the bleeps that we use when we do the show on the radio. If you prefer a bleeped version of the show, it's at our website. Now here's Dana.
<v Ira Glass>开始前有个快速警告，因为这是我们节目的播客互联网版，我们去掉了在电台广播时使用的消音提示音。如果你更喜欢消音版，可以在我们的网站上找到。现在有请达娜。

00:05:49.750 --> 00:06:01.120
<v Dana Chivvis>We've been talking about this podcast for five minutes already. And the whole point of the podcast is to tell crazy drug stories, so let's get right to one. This is from the very first episode. Chris tells the story.
<v Dana Chivvis>我们谈论这个播客已经五分钟了。而播客的全部意义在于讲述疯狂的药物故事，所以我们直接来一个。这是第一集里的。克里斯讲的故事。

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:12.580
<v Chris>So I relapsed. I don't even want to say I relapsed. I just started using again after a brief period of abstinence. And I took a lot of Xanax-- about 60 bars-- for those of you that know bars.
<v Chris>所以我复吸了。我甚至不想说我复吸了。只是在短暂的戒断期后我又开始用了。我吃了很多赞安诺（Xanax）——大约60片（bar），对于那些知道bar是什么的人来说。

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:13.470
<v Dave>At once?
<v Dave>一次吃掉？

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.220
<v Chris>No, over a period of about three or four days.
<v Chris>不，在大约三四天的时间里。

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.250
<v Dave>So you took 120 milligrams over four days?
<v Dave>所以你在四天内吃了120毫克？

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:39.820
<v Chris>Yeah, and I blacked out for about a week. And during that blackout, I robbed a veterinarian. I went into the veterinary clinic. I told them my cat was having seizures and I needed phenobarbital ASAP. They told me something like, you know, we don't just hand out medications for the asking. Please fill out some paperwork and can we see your cat? Obviously, I had no cat with me.
<v Chris>对，我断片了大约一周。在断片期间，我抢劫了一家兽医诊所。我走进诊所，告诉他们我的猫在抽搐，我需要尽快获得苯巴比妥。他们说了一些话，大意是，我们不会随随便便把药物给别人。请填一下表格，让我们看看你的猫？显然，我没带猫。

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:41.200
<v Dave>Did you have a cat at home?
<v Dave>你家里有猫吗？

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.730
<v Chris>I did have a cat at home, Smegal.
<v Chris>家里是有只猫，叫斯米戈尔（Smegal）。

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.740
<v Chris>[LAUGHING]
<v Chris>[笑声]

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:57.430
<v Chris>So I fill out the paperwork. And while I was filling out the paperwork, I ended up putting it down and storming into the back, knocked over the nurse. And the veterinarian came into the medication storage room and tried to stop me.
<v Chris>所以我填了表格。在填表时，我把它放下，冲进了后台，撞倒了护士。兽医跑进储药室试图阻止我。

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:06.210
<v Chris>I got out. The police came. I struggled with the police and fought them, and got a bunch of assaults on the police officers. There was a helicopter on the scene. It was a big, big deal.
<v Chris>我逃了出来。警察来了。我和警察发生了肢体冲突，还袭警了。现场还有直升机。这事闹得非常大。

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:20.410
<v Dave>And just so you know something about Chris-- he's burly. Chris is at least six feet. He's like one of these Massachusetts Protestant white guys. He's very big and broad. And I would be scared if Chris got upset.
<v Dave>为了让你了解克里斯——他很魁梧。他至少六英尺高。他就像那种马萨诸塞州的白人清教徒大个子。如果克里斯生气，我会害怕的。

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:21.550
<v Chris>But I'm a gentle person.
<v Chris>但我是一个温和的人。

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.310
<v Dave>Very gentle, very gentle soul-- like a lamb.
<v Dave>非常温和，非常温柔的灵魂——就像羔羊一样。

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:35.950
<v Dana Chivvis>If Chris is the lamb in a drug addicts clothing, Dave is the border collie, constantly circling Chris, herding him between drug stories, nipping at his ankles. For his Boston accent--
<v Dana Chivvis>如果克里斯是穿着瘾君子外衣的羔羊，戴夫就是那只边境牧羊犬，不断绕着克里斯转，把他从药物故事中赶回来，咬他的脚后跟。关于他的波士顿口音——

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:39.640
<v Dave>Oh you invite all your Facebook friends to like Dopey.
<v Dave>哦，你邀请了你所有的Facebook好友来点赞《Dopey》。

